# BOTH FORMS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS NOT REDUNDANT. `node_modules/` matches a
# DIRECTORY only, so a SYMLINK named node_modules is a blob (mode 120000) and slips
# straight past it -- which is exactly what happened while V8 was built in a git
# worktree: `ln -s ../../node_modules` to avoid a second install, and `git add -A`
# committed an ABSOLUTE-PATH symlink into fifteen commits. Nothing was pushed, so it
# was rewritten out; the point of this line is that it cannot recur.
#
# Why it matters beyond tidiness: checking out any such commit -- a `git bisect`, a
# look at an old revision -- silently creates a node_modules symlink pointing at
# another checkout's dependencies.
node_modules/
node_modules

# Next.js
.next/
out/
next-env.d.ts
.vercel/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

*.log
npm-debug.*
.DS_Store

# Secrets. LLM_API_KEY, AUTH_SECRET and AUTH_USERS live here.
# `.env.example` is committed; nothing matching these patterns ever is.
.env
.env.local
.env*.local

# Google hands you the OAuth client as a download named client_secret_<id>.json.
# Nothing in this project reads it -- the two values it holds belong in
# .env.local -- but the default download lands in whatever directory you were
# in, and without this line the repo root is a plausible one. A committed
# client secret means rotating the OAuth client and re-registering every
# redirect URI.
client_secret*.json

# Local harnesses that plant a session cookie so headless Chrome, or a
# same-origin iframe, can reach gated pages. They live under public/cards/
# because that path is excluded from the middleware matcher -- which is the only
# reason they load at all, and also the reason they must never be committed:
# anything served from public/ is served to the internet.
#
# They are only useful with DEV_PASSWORD_LOGIN=1, which cannot be on in a
# deployment, so a leaked one would be inert rather than an open door. Ignored
# anyway: "inert because of a flag somewhere else" is not a property to bet a
# login on.
public/cards/_seed.html
public/cards/_*.html

# Source art is kept, but the normalized output is generated by
# tools/normalize_cards.py -- keep it committed so Vercel builds without Python.
tools/_contactsheet.jpg
# Same, for tools/make_wallpapers.py. public/wallpapers/ IS committed, for the
# same reason public/cards/ is; only the eyeballing sheet is scratch.
tools/_wallpapersheet.jpg
# Same, for tools/make_avatars.py. public/readers/ IS committed; the 4x contact
# strip only exists so the three crop boxes can be reviewed by eye.
tools/_avatarsheet.jpg

# git worktrees
.worktrees/

.vercel
.env*
# ...but .env.example is the documentation for every variable and is committed.
# `.env*` would otherwise silently swallow it, and any sibling added later.
!.env.example

# Card-art candidates from /generate-tarot-card. Scratch by definition: 3MB PNGs,
# many per card, and only the promoted one matters. Promotion copies it up into
# assets/major_arcanas/, which IS committed.
assets/major_arcanas/_candidates/

# tools/check_card_art.py imports normalize_cards.py, which creates this.
__pycache__/

# EnterWorktree creates isolated worktrees here. Never part of a commit.
.claude/worktrees/
